/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Both link types use the same chain of blocks.
   - Every loss before the photodiode lowers the current, but the preamplifier's thermal noise does not shrink with it, so received power is the first thing a design checks.
   - A digital link design starts from three requirements: the bit rate B in bit/s, the distance L in km, and the bit error rate (BER) the user will accept.
   - The designer then chooses the parts; some syllabi call these the system considerations:
   - An 850 nm LED on multimode fiber with a silicon pin detector is cheapest for a few kilometres at tens of Mbit/s; a 1310 nm laser on single-mode fiber covers tens of kilometres; a 1550 nm DFB laser with an APD or amplifier covers long spans.
   - Every design then faces two checks.
   - In an analog link the light intensity follows the signal: the laser is biased at a steady power and the signal swings it up and down, so the source must be linear.
   - The photocurrent has a mean Ip = ℛP and a signal of peak mIp, whose mean-square value is (mIp)²/2.
   - P is mean received power in W; RIN is the laser's relative intensity noise per Hz (−143 dB/Hz = 10−14.3/Hz); B is bandwidth in Hz; q = 1.602 × 10⁻¹⁹ C; ID is dark current; kB = 1.381 × 10⁻²³ J/K; T in K; Ft is the preamplifier noise figure 
   - RIN noise grows as P², like the signal, so it sets a CNR ceiling that more power cannot lift.
*/
