/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - PT is the mean power launched into the fiber, in dBm.
   - PT is launched power in dBm; PR is receiver sensitivity in dBm; α is fiber attenuation in dB/km; L is fiber length in km; lc is the loss per connector in dB and Nc the number of connectors; ls is the loss per splice in dB and Ns the number 
   - Both sides are in dB.
   - The received power is PT minus the physical losses only, in dBm.
   - The receiver has a ceiling too: overload, the largest power before the preamplifier saturates.
   - Use planning values, not the best laboratory number:
   - Count joints carefully: connectors at both ends and at every patch panel; one splice between reels, so 60 km in 10 km reels has five splices.
   - Write the budget as a table, losses in the order the light meets them; here for the worked example's 60 km link:
   - If the spare is negative, cutting M to make it fit only hides risk.
   - Whether the pulses are still sharp at that distance is the job of the rise-time budget; the link must pass both.
*/
